            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Solon's Early Life</strong><br>\nSolon was born into a noble family in Athens around 638 BCE. Despite his noble birth, his family was not wealthy, and Solon had to work for a living. He traveled widely, which exposed him to new ideas and cultures. His experiences would later influence his work as a lawmaker and leader in Athens. He gained a reputation as a wise man, and his poetry reflected his thoughts on Athenian society and politics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Power in Athens</strong><br>\nIn the early 6th century BCE, Athens was suffering from severe economic, social, and political problems. The city was divided between the rich and the poor, with the former enslaving those who could not pay their debts. In 594 BCE, the Athenians chose Solon as their chief magistrate, or Archon, with special powers to solve these problems and prevent the looming civil war.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Solon's Economic Reforms</strong><br>\nSolon implemented a series of economic reforms known as the \"Shaking off of Burdens,\" which aimed to relieve the suffering of the poor. He canceled all debts, forbade debt slavery, and liberated those who had been enslaved. These reforms helped to stabilize the Athenian economy and alleviate the crisis, although they did not entirely eliminate wealth inequality.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
