            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Political and Legal Reforms</strong><br>\nSolon reorganized the Athenian political system to reflect wealth rather than birth. He created four income classes that determined citizens' political opportunities and responsibilities. He also established the Council of Four Hundred to guide the city's policies and created courts staffed by citizens to ensure that the rule of law was upheld over the rule of the elite.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Introduction of the Heliaia</strong><br>\nThe Heliaia was a popular court introduced by Solon and served as a precursor to democratic judicial institutions. Any citizen could bring a complaint before this court, providing an early form of public trial and jury system. The Heliaia helped to democratize justice, making it more accessible to the common Athenian.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Creation of the Solonian Constitution</strong><br>\nThe Solonian Constitution was an important step towards democracy in Athens. While it did not establish a full democracy, it laid the foundation for further democratic developments by Cleisthenes and others. Solon's constitution relaxed the stringent class structure and allowed more participation by the common people in government decisions.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
